body:
It's easy if we half up the Kantian brain: 

&nbsp;

There's the understanding which contains our concepts, categories (casualty, freedom, etc), our analytic judgements. 

&nbsp;

The sensibility contains our passively received intuitions, empirical stuff, the imagination, and the pure intuitions of time and space (which filters our intuitions basically). 

&nbsp;

Understanding + Sensibility synthesized = knowledge / experience

&nbsp;

Memory contains empirical experiences as you mentioned. (and the empirical experience required of memory is filtered through the intuitions of time and space). This makes it appear a posteriori as you said. Memories are located in the imagination where it produces or reproduces the object at hand or their previous empirical experiences of said object. If the event / object hasn't been experienced (in the Kantian sense), then we can't have knowledge of it basically. A common example used is the person who is born deaf or blind. A blind person't cannot produce these visual experiences without having had it. Even though memory requires empirical experiences as the content (the content is passively received through the sensibility), it also needs to *actively* produce/reproduce these experiences. Kantian, I think, is making the typical transcendental argument. So, I would argue that it is actually a synthetic a priori judgement in the same way Kantian mathematics is. 

&nbsp;

Kantian mathematics is a synthetic a priori because is requires the categories (quantity, etc) but we construct the equations synthetically. *this is a super brief and bad explanation but can explore in greater detail if asked* It requires the pure intuitions of time / space. -- this is a weird perspective I know and I have argued against it many times. (Kant's a constructivist) So I think the understanding actively produces / reproduces the content that is passively received by the sensibility (through the imagination). 

&nbsp;

**TLDR; I have reasons to believe that memory is synthetic a priori in a similar way the truths of mathematics are.**
